{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_129","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git grep -n -E 'point.*window|automatic.*append|aria-valuetext' d9fa4b0 -- docs/decisions | head -10\"","aggregated_output":"d9fa4b0:docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md:11:| C. One native `<input type=\"range\">` laid over the plot, its value the position along x, its `aria-valuetext` the point's text | A native control: desktop readers enter focus mode on it by themselves, arrow keys, Home, End and Page keys work without script, and a change of value text is read without a live region. Touch screen readers expose a native range as an adjustable element, so swiping up and down moves through points (from memory for VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack; not verified here). One element whatever the number of points. | It is announced as a slider, which is not what a chart is; mitigated by `aria-roledescription` and a label that says what moving it does. Up and Down natively do the same as Right and Left. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
