r an automatically updating compact tile. I4’s scroll test covers full charts only. `docs/02-product-spec.md:128–130,203`, `B/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:843–864`\n- **L1 and L5 compact.** Compact still displays state text that can clip, and isolated points still need markers. Routing those obligations to L7 and F2/C2 is reasonable if the cells identify the applicable subclauses and cite their evidence. `docs/02-product-spec.md:112,116,208`, `B/compact.browser.test.ts:161–169`\n- **DX1 compact.** `compact: true` explains the extra required option. It does not dispose of the ten-line npm and script-tag requirements. `docs/02-product-spec.md:203,223`\n\n## 4. WCAG citations\n\nThe generated conformance table attaches **every primary test from every type in a product row** to each WCAG number cited by that row. For example, A4’s million-row jump would appear under an input-label criterion even though it locates the field by CSS class. A correct row-to-WCAG reference still needs a test-to-WCAG reference. `apps/docs/lib/conformance.mjs:128–137,188–195` [WCAG 2.2 Recommendation](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n| Spec row | Judgment on every current citation |\n|---|---|\n| **F12** | **4.1.3 — remove as presently evidenced or narrow to a status-role test.** The cited helper accepts ordinary static text. An existing line test explicitly requires `role=\"status\"` and its accessibility-tree representation. `B/f12-note.browser.test.ts:20–27`, `H/data.browser.test.ts:18–27` |\n| **L2** | **1.4.3 and 1.4.11 — keep.** Text/background and mark, axis and focus-indicator contrast directly address them. Limit credit to the sampled surfaces and schemes. Tooltip, table and state-message text are material omissions. `B/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:242` |\n| **L3** | **1.3.4 — remove.** Container resizing and becoming visible establish responsive sizing. They never exercise a portrait/landscape restriction on viewing or operation. Record orientation as unassessed until there is evidence for it. `docs/02-product-spec.md:114` [WCAG Orientation](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#orientation) |\n| **L6** | **2.4.6 — keep.** Descriptive axis titles, field names, units and reference labels address the purpose of labels. The meaningfulness of author-supplied wording remains the author’s responsibility. `B/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:557–593` |\n| **I1** | **1.4.13 — keep, scoped.** Hovering onto the tooltip, retaining its contents and dismissing with Escape are asserted. **2.4.11 — keep as supporting evidence.** The keyboard also triggers the overlap check, but the helper skips it if there is no visible tooltip. A2 provides the stronger focus-specific assertion. `B/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:167–206,295–324` |\n| **I2** | **2.5.3 and 4.1.2 — keep** for the tested legend control. Its visible label, computed name, button role and pressed-state changes correspond to the criteria. Add **2.1.1** for Enter and Space. `B/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:534–552` |\n| **I3** | **2.5.1 — keep** for the single-pointer alternative to pinch. **2.5.7 — use I4 as primary** for selecting an arbitrary range with two clicks. Fixed-factor zoom buttons alone do not reproduce that operation. **1.4.4 — move primary evidence to L1** and retain modified-wheel handling only as supporting evidence. **2.1.4 — remove as covered** until the test establishes that a character shortcut is focus-scoped, disabled or remappable. Pressing it while focused establishes none of those protections. `B/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:683,742–748`, `B/l-legibility.browser.test.ts:110–127` [WCAG Character Key Shortcuts](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#character-key-shortcuts) |\n| **I4** | **2.5.7 and 2.5.8 — keep** for the two-click range operation and the targets actually measured. The jump field, pager and state-dependent controls lack complete size coverage. Add **2.5.1** for the single-pointer alternative, and **2.5.3 / 4.1.2** for the named Select range toggle and its state. `B/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts: